    "data": {
      "A": "Research outer-loop CPU parallelism. Assign contiguous ranges of target bodies i to at most four workers, keeping each body's j traversal and arithmetic unchanged. Positions must remain immutable throughout force evaluation; synchronize before position integration and before the next iteration's force evaluation. Investigate persistent workers across all ten iterations to reduce launch overhead, using only threading facilities supported by the unchanged build. Compare against a serial fallback for small workloads, with any dispatch based on general workload size rather than test identities. Deliver a correctness-validated standalone program and measured timing evidence.",
      "B": "Research instruction-level parallelism and ARM64 SIMD across independent target bodies, not reductions across source bodies. Process small target blocks with separate force accumulators, broadcasting each source position while preserving ascending j order for every target. Compare scalar interleaving with supported SIMD implementations and modest register-block sizes. Preserve the float squared-distance computation, promotion to double for sqrt and reciprocal, conversion back to float, and the existing build's contraction behavior. Do not substitute approximate reciprocal square roots, sqrtf, or reassociated sums. Handle all block tails correctly. Deliver a standalone kernel with evidence distinguishing useful throughput gains from register-pressure regressions.",
      "C": "Research memory layout and timestep dataflow. Compare the existing array of structures with persistent separate position and velocity arrays, converting only at input/output boundaries. Investigate cache blocking that preserves each body's source accumulation order. Separately evaluate fusing velocity and position updates by writing new positions into a second buffer while all force calculations read immutable old positions; publish or swap buffers only after every target is complete. Never overwrite positions still needed by another target. Measure whether reduced passes and improved locality outweigh conversion, buffering, and allocation costs. Keep arbitrary valid body counts, checked storage sizing, and a safe fallback where appropriate.",
      "G": "Integrate actual validated programs produced by A/B/C. First compare standalone results, then evaluate selected combinations within the existing budget; threading, SIMD, layout, and buffering gains are not necessarily additive. In particular, recheck synchronization when combining persistent workers with buffered position updates, and retune only justified block-size or workload-size choices. Preserve the input integer body-count header and the output consisting solely of six floats per body in original order. Run the unchanged correctness and timing procedures, checking available coverage of tails and numerical edge cases. Report measured mean runtime and speedup against the 0.663898-second baseline and strongest standalone candidate. Retain the strongest validated program if integration regresses; change no tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, or permissions.",
      "analysis": "The relevant problem is the supplied N-body source, not the other contest problems. Its dominant algorithmic work is ten all-pairs force evaluations, O(10 n²), followed by linear position updates. The measured baseline is valid on four correctness cases and averages 0.663898 seconds across three closely grouped samples; no optimized measurements, workload sizes, or profiling evidence are supplied. Four ARM64 CPU cores and no GPU motivate complementary research in target-body threading, order-preserving SIMD, and memory/dataflow improvements. Numerical compatibility is central: sqrt and reciprocal are evaluated in double before conversion to float, while force accumulation is ordered in float. Preserve all interactions, softening, timestep, iteration count, and reference-compatible arithmetic. Passing four cases alone does not establish correctness for every valid input."
    },
